    probabilityStatsIntro: 'Probability and statistics are the mathematical foundation of successful trading. Understanding these concepts allows you to make rational data-driven decisions, not emotional ones.',
    mathematicalExpectationTitle: 'Mathematical Expectation',
    expectationFormulaTitle: 'Expectation Formula',
    expectationFormulaDesc: 'E = (Win% × Avg Win) - (Loss% × Avg Loss). This is THE most important metric. If E > 0, your strategy is profitable long-term. Example: 50% win rate, avg win $200, avg loss $100 → E = (0.5 × 200) - (0.5 × 100) = $50 per trade.',
    whyExpectationMattersTitle: 'Why It\'s The Key Metric',
    whyExpectationMattersDesc: 'You can have 30% accuracy and be profitable if your wins are 3x your losses. Or have 70% accuracy and lose money if your losses are larger than wins. Expectation summarizes everything.',
    lawOfLargeNumbersTitle: 'Law of Large Numbers',
    largeNumbersConceptTitle: 'Concept',
    largeNumbersConceptDesc: 'Your edge only manifests with enough trades. 10 trades aren\'t enough. 100 trades start being representative. 300+ trades give statistical confidence. Short-term, luck dominates. Long-term, probability converges.',
    varianceShortTermTitle: 'Short-Term Variance',
    varianceShortTermDesc: 'Even with 60% win rate, you can have 5-7 consecutive losses. This does NOT mean your strategy doesn\'t work. It\'s normal variance. That\'s why capital management matters: to survive bad streaks.',
    resultsDistributionTitle: 'Results Distribution',
    normalDistributionTitle: 'Reality vs Theory',
    normalDistributionDesc: 'Books assume normal distribution (bell curve). Trading reality has "fat tails": extreme events happen more often than expected. Prepare for black swans (unexpected crashes).',
