    basicsMicroTitle: 'Micro Lot (0.01)',
    basicsMicroUnits: '1,000 units',
    basicsMicroDesc: 'The minimum unit available at most brokers. Ideal for learning without destroying the account. With 10 pips against you, you only lose $1. Perfect for testing strategies in live markets with minimal risk.',
    basicsMicroTip: 'Use it to validate strategies in the live market with controlled risk.',
    basicsMiniTitle: 'Mini Lot (0.1)',
    basicsMiniUnits: '10,000 units',
    basicsMiniDesc: '10 times the micro lot. The standard size for growing accounts. Allows diversification across multiple simultaneous positions without overexposing yourself.',
    basicsMiniTip: 'The ideal entry point for intermediate traders with $1,000–$10,000.',
    basicsStdTitle: 'Standard Lot (1.0)',
    basicsStdUnits: '100,000 units',
    basicsStdDesc: 'The reference unit in the Forex market. Each pip moves $10 in EUR/USD. With a 50-pip SL you risk $500 — you need at least $25,000–$50,000 to risk only 1–2% per trade.',
    basicsStdWarning: 'Do not use standard lots with small accounts: the risk per trade will be excessive.',
    basicsMacroTitle: 'Macro / Big (10+ lots)',
    basicsMacroUnits: '1M+ units',
    basicsMacroDesc: 'Institutional territory. Banks, funds and hedge funds operate at this size. With 100 lots, each pip is worth $1,000. These positions often move the price and create the supports/resistances we see on the chart.',
    basicsMacroInfo: 'Knowing these sizes helps understand why price respects certain levels.',
    basicsPosFormulaTitle: 'How to calculate your optimal position size',
    basicsPosFormulaLabel: 'Universal formula',
    basicsPosFormula: 'Lots = (Capital × %Risk) ÷ (SL_Pips × Pip_Value)',
    basicsPosFormulaExLabel: 'Practical example:',
    basicsPosFormulaExDesc: 'Capital: $5,000 | Risk: 1% | SL: 50 pips',
    basicsPosFormulaExResult: 'Lots = ($5,000 × 0.01) ÷ (50 × $1) = 1.0 mini lot',
    basicsPosGuideTitle: 'Quick guide by account size',
    basicsPosGuideNote: '* Assuming SL of 100 pips in EUR/USD',
    basicsPosWarningTitle: 'The most common mistake: oversizing the position',
    basicsPosWarning: 'Opening with 1 standard lot because "the move looks safe" when your account has $2,000 means a 20-pip SL (−$200) makes you lose 10% of your capital in a single trade. Always calculate the lot size from the maximum allowed risk (1–2%), never the other way around.',
    basicsFaqPosTitle: 'Frequently asked questions — Position Size',
    basicsFaqPos1Q: 'What is a lot in trading?',
    basicsFaqPos1A: 'A lot is the standard unit of measure for position size in Forex and CFDs. 1 standard lot equals 100,000 units of the base currency. This standardisation makes it easy to calculate the pip value and the risk of each trade.',
    basicsFaqPos2Q: 'What lot size should I start with?',
    basicsFaqPos2A: 'If your account has less than $1,000, start with micro lots (0.01). If you have between $1,000 and $10,000, use mini lots (0.1). Standard lots require accounts of $10,000+ for sensible risk management (risking only 1–2% per trade).',
    basicsFaqPos3Q: 'How does leverage affect position size?',
    basicsFaqPos3A: 'Leverage allows you to control a standard lot (100,000 units) with much less capital. With 1:100 you only need $1,000 of margin for 1 lot. But leverage amplifies both gains and losses: 1 lot with 1:100 leverage in EUR/USD is $10/pip of profit OR loss.',
    basicsFaqPos4Q: 'What is pip value and how is it calculated?',
    basicsFaqPos4A: 'The pip is the minimum price movement (0.0001 in EUR/USD). Pip value = (0.0001 × lots × 100,000) in the quoted currency. In EUR/USD: 1 standard lot → $10/pip | 1 mini lot → $1/pip | 1 micro lot → $0.10/pip.',
    basicsFaqPos5Q: 'Why do institutions use large lots?',
    basicsFaqPos5A: 'Investment funds, banks and hedge funds move hundreds of millions of dollars. With 100 standard lots (10 million units) each pip is worth $1,000. At that scale, moving the price a few pips means tens of thousands in PnL. That is why their entries and exits often appear as supports/resistances on the chart.',
    basicsSummaryTitle: 'Summary: the 5 concepts together',
    basicsSummaryLongDesc: 'You buy expecting the price to rise',
    basicsSummaryShortDesc: 'You sell expecting the price to fall',
    basicsSummaryTpDesc: 'Closes with profit when the target is reached',
    basicsSummarySlDesc: 'Closes with a controlled loss if you are wrong',
    basicsSummaryLotsTitle: 'LOTS',
    basicsSummaryLotsDesc: 'Micro · Mini · Standard · Macro',

