    slGuideTitle: 'Stop-Loss: Fixed vs. Variable',
    slGuideIntro: 'Where you place the stop decides your risk, your R multiple and ultimately your expectancy. A stop placed by market structure adapts to reality; an arbitrary fixed stop fights it.',
    slTypesTitle: 'Three ways to place a stop',
    slStructureName: 'Structure-based',
    slStructureDesc: 'The stop sits just beyond the swing high/low, support or resistance that would invalidate your idea. The market tells you where you\'re wrong — adaptive and logical.',
    slAtrName: 'Volatility-based (ATR)',
    slAtrDesc: 'Stop distance = a multiple of ATR (e.g. 1.5×ATR). It widens in volatile regimes and tightens in calm ones, so you aren\'t stopped out by normal noise.',
    slFixedName: 'Fixed (pips / %)',
    slFixedDesc: 'Always the same distance (e.g. 20 pips or 2%) regardless of structure or volatility. Simple, but it ignores where the trade is actually invalidated — and over a large sample that quietly erodes expectancy.',
    slBreakevenTitle: 'The minimum R:R is NOT universal',
    slBreakevenIntro: 'The break-even reward:risk depends entirely on your win rate. A high-accuracy system is profitable below 1:1; a low-accuracy trend system needs a large R. \'Always use 1:2\' is a myth.',
    slColWinRate: 'Win Rate',
    slColBreakeven: 'Break-even R:R',
    slColProfitable: 'Profitable above',
    slBreakevenFormula: 'Break-even R = (1 − WinRate) / WinRate',
    slKeyInsight: 'A fixed arbitrary stop forces a fixed R that may not match your real win rate — so even a \'good\' setup can carry negative expectancy. Place the stop where the idea is wrong, size down to keep risk constant, and let R:R follow from structure.',
    otGuideTitle: 'The Order Book & Order Types',
    otGuideIntro: 'Every trade is matched in the order book: a live ladder of resting buy and sell orders. Understanding it explains spread, liquidity and slippage — and the hidden orders institutions use.',
